# Heimdall Catallaxy

A personal, local-only market/macro dashboard (static HTML/CSS/JS, no server, no build step,
opened via `file://` and also deployed to GitHub Pages). Purpose: check BTC, rates, Fed/liquidity,
inflation, dollar/FX, credit, energy, and AI-buildout signals in ~30 seconds, then drill into a
tab. It is explicitly **not** a day-trading terminal, a news aggregator, or a metrics dump — every
card/chart/indicator must earn its place. Signal over quantity, always.

## Non-negotiable data rules

- **Fail stale, never broken.** If a fetch fails, returns malformed data, or has nothing new,
  retain the last known-good cached value. Never blank, zero, or show a broken-looking state
  when valid cached data still exists. "No new data" (weekends, holidays, unchanged prints) is
  expected, not an error — don't manufacture change.
- **Never fabricate data.** No plausible-looking placeholder numbers, ever. Missing = "—" and
  say so, not a guessed value.
- **Never coerce missing values to zero.** Null/NaN/empty is "missing," not "0".
- **Validate before overwrite, write atomically.** Every `fetch_*.ps1` script dot-sources
  `fetch_common.ps1` for this — `Test-SeriesSane`, `Get-ValidatedMergedSeries`,
  `Write-DataFileAtomic`. Use these, don't hand-roll new validate/merge/write logic.
- **Full-history refetch, not incremental**, for time-series fetches — every run pulls the
  entire available history from source, so a gap in refresh runs never creates a gap in data.
  Live cross-sectional snapshots (CME basis, the GEX options-chain snapshot) are the one
  exception — those aren't a mergeable series, so on failure just leave the existing cached
  snapshot file untouched rather than merging/trimming.
- **Verify a source's literal units before scaling anything.** Don't assume from a field name.
  Real bugs already happened here: EIA `sales` field is million kWh (not GWh, no scaling needed,
  a same-session bug divided by 1000 anyway), a nominal-vs-real GDP mismatch, an inverted
  purchasing-power formula, a misread FRED "as of" date.

## Reuse over duplication (hard rule)

One shared component per concern, reused by every tab that needs it — never let a page roll its
own version:
- `charts.js` — time-series line chart (range toggle, log toggle, CSV export)
- `format.js` — `applyTooltip`/`obsTooltip` (standard tooltip), `downloadCsv`/`downloadSeriesCsv`
- `nav.js` — tab bar, rendered from one `PAGES` array (add a tab here, not per-HTML-file), plus
  the keyboard shortcuts (←/→ step tabs, Home→Summary, B→BTC)
- `ticker.js` — the live BTC price ticker. Also mirrors its validated price into any element
  tagged `.btc-live-mirror` anywhere on the page (single source of truth, no per-page polling),
  and syncs across open browser tabs instantly via the `storage` event instead of each tab
  drifting on its own poll timer.
- `fetch_common.ps1` — shared validate/merge/atomic-write library for every fetch script

A genuinely different chart *shape* (e.g. the GEX by-strike bar chart, which is categorical, not
time-series) is a legitimate reason to write a standalone Chart.js config — don't force it through
`charts.js`. Reuse applies to same-shape things, not as a rule to distort a different chart into.

## Tooltip convention (do not break)

Every number's hover tooltip ends with exactly: `Observation: <date> · Heimdall checked: <fetch
time>`. If a card already has other tooltip content (a calc-methodology explanation, etc.), that
explanation stays first and the provenance note is **appended after it**, never prepended. Use
`HeimdallFormat.applyTooltip(labelEl, obsDate, freq, generatedAtUtc)` — don't hand-roll this.

## Modeled/estimated metrics need a `calc` badge

Anything Heimdall computes rather than sources directly (Net Liquidity Proxy on the Fed tab,
Dealer Gamma Exposure on the BTC tab) gets a `calc-badge` and a methodology tooltip explaining
the assumption it rests on. Never present a derived estimate as if it were an observed fact —
e.g. GEX's "dealers net long calls/short puts" is a standard industry convention, not verified
dealer positioning, and the UI says so.

## PowerShell 5.1 gotchas (real bugs already caught here — don't reintroduce)

- `Write-Output` inside a function becomes part of its **return value**. Use `Write-Host` for
  diagnostic/log lines inside any function whose return value matters.
- `ConvertFrom-Json -Depth N` is **not supported on Windows PowerShell 5.1** — omit `-Depth`.
- `$var = , (pipeline)` on a **plain assignment** double-wraps the result. The leading comma is
  only correct inside a `return` statement (to stop PowerShell unrolling an array return value).
- Every `Invoke-RestMethod`/`Invoke-WebRequest` call needs `-TimeoutSec 30` (or similar) — one
  hung call with no timeout has already caused a real test run to hang.
- `Sort-Object -Property <name> -Descending` on an array of **hashtables** can silently sort
  ascending. Use `[PSCustomObject]` instead of `@{}`, or do a manual max-find loop.
- `key = (if (cond) {a} else {b})` — parenthesized if-expression inside a hashtable literal —
  fails to parse. Drop the wrapping parens.
- GitHub Actions cron is fixed UTC with no DST awareness — see `.github/workflows/refresh.yml`
  for the pattern (fire at both EDT/EST UTC equivalents, gate at runtime with DST-aware
  `TimeZoneInfo`, `America/New_York` on Linux runners with a `Eastern Standard Time` fallback
  for portability). Windows Task Scheduler triggers don't need this — they track local wall
  clock (incl. DST) natively.

## New data sources

Check FRED first — it covers most macro/Fed data for free. If a genuinely new source is needed
and requires an API key, that's the user's call to register for, never done autonomously. If a
source has **no free/self-serve tier at all** (confirmed for CME CF BRRNY and FINRA TRACE — both
licensed/paid-only), say so plainly and propose a free alternative rather than quietly
substituting one or scraping around the paywall. A scraped HTML page (no API contract, breaks
silently on redesign) is a last resort, not a default — flag it as fragile before building on it.

## Verification bar

Load the actual change in a browser before calling it done — read the diff is not enough. This
project's dev loop: a scratchpad `test_server.ps1` (HttpListener on :8843, needed for `http://`
vs `file://` load-order differences during dev), check console for errors, and assert
structurally (element text, chart existence) rather than trusting a screenshot alone. For
anything involving retry/backoff/fail-stale logic, actually simulate the failure (mock `fetch`,
point a script at a dead endpoint) rather than reasoning about it abstractly — this has caught
real bugs that code review alone would have missed.

## Don't, without explicit instruction

Redesign the whole site, change the nav structure wholesale, migrate frameworks, replace a
working data source casually, add authentication/backend/database, add AI-generated commentary
or trading recommendations, or add a metric just because a source makes it available. When in
doubt: preserve and refine, smallest coherent change, ask rather than guess on anything
consequential or irreversible.
